t of the Nobel Prize in Economic Sciences | University of Chicago<br /> Chicago, Illinois</span><br/></div></div><div class="row"><div class="col-12 col-lg-6"><div class="i9-c-person-block--small " data-wf="person Block (Small)"><div class="i9-c-person-block--small__image--container"><a class="i9-c-person-block--small__image--wrapper" href="/people/narayana-kocherlakota"><img alt="photo of Narayana Kocherlakota" loading="lazy" width="400" height="400" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="i9-c-person-block--small__image js-lazy" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/-/jssmedia/assets/people/narayana-kocherlakota/nrk-photo.jpg?rev=062adb181d7443ba8b73aac434c09c78&amp;mw=640 1x, /-/jssmedia/assets/people/narayana-kocherlakota/nrk-photo.jpg?rev=062adb181d7443ba8b73aac434c09c78&amp;mw=828 2x" src="/-/jssmedia/assets/people/narayana-kocherlakota/nrk-photo.jpg?rev=062adb181d7443ba8b73aac434c09c78&amp;mw=828"/></a></div><div class="i9-c-person-block--small__content--container"><div class="i9-c-person-block--small__content--name"><a href="/people/narayana-kocherlakota">Narayana Kocherlakota</a><small class="i9-c-person-block--small__content--position">President</small></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="responsive-image-wrapper"><img alt="" width="1397" height="996" sizes="(max-width: 576px) 576px, (max-width: 768px) 768px, 992px" class="i9-u-container-fluid__background--image" fetchpriority="high" srcSet="/-/jssmedia/assets/backgroundimages/mfed-banner-bg.jpg?mw=576&amp;rev=fbb02f37035245a6a861d687ab8abcc7 576w, /-/jssmedia/assets/backgroundimages/mfed-banner-bg.jpg?mw=768&amp;rev=fbb02f37035245a6a861d687ab8abcc7 768w, /-/jssmedia/assets/backgroundimages/mfed-banner-bg.jpg?mw=992&amp;rev=fbb02f37035245a6a861d687ab8abcc7 992w" src="/-/media/assets/backgroundimages/mfed-banner-bg.jpg?h=996&amp;iar=0&amp;w=1397&amp;rev=fbb02f37035245a6a861d687ab8abcc7"/></div></div><div class="i9-c-article-sticky-header__container i9-u-background-primary container-fluid container-fluid-no-overlow" data-wf="Article Sticky Header" style="top:-200px"><div class="row"><div class="container"><div class="i9-c-article-sticky-header"><div class="i9-c-article-sticky-header__title h3" data-ce="false">Lessons from Lars</div></div></div></div><div class="i9-c-article-sticky-header__progress-bar i9-js-article-sticky-header__progress-bar i9-u-background-dark-gradient-noangle"></div></div><div class="i9-u-padding-top i9-u-padding-bottom container"><div class="row"><div class="offset-lg-1"></div><div id="social-start" class="col-12 col-lg-1 d-lg-block"><div class="i9-c-social-share-block"><div class="i9-c-social-share-block__title">Share</div><div class="i9-c-social-icon-block"><a class="i9-c-social-icon" href="/speeches/2013/lessons-from-lars#"><svg version="1.1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" x="0px" y="0px" width="96.124px" height="96.123px" viewBox="0 0 96.124 96.123" xml:space="preserve" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true" aria-label="facebook"><g><path d="M72.089,0.02L59.624,0C45.62,0,36.57,9.285,36.57,23.656v10.907H24.037c-1.083,0-1.96,0.878-1.96,1.961v15.803   c0,1.083,0.878,1.96,1.96,1.96h12.533v39.876c0,1.083,0.877,1.96,1.96,1.96h16.352c1.083,0,1.96-0.878,1.96-1.96V54.287h14.654   c1.083,0,1.96-0.877,1.96-1.96l0.006-15.803c0-0.52-0.207-1.018-0.574-1.386c-0.367-0.368-0.867-0.575-1.387-0.575H56.842v-9.246   c0-4.444,1.059-6.7,6.848-6.7l8.397-0.003c1.082,0,1.959-0.878,1.959-1.96V1.98C74.046,0.899,73.17,0.022,72.089,0.02z"></path></g></svg><span class="sr-only">Facebook</span></a><a class="i9-c-social-icon" href="/speeches/2013/lessons-from-lars#"><svg version="1.1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" x="0px" y="0px" width="430.117px" height="430.117px" viewBox="0 0 430.117 430.117" xml:space="preserve" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true" aria-label="linkedin"><g><path d="M430.117,261.543V420.56h-92.188V272.193c0-37.271-13.334-62.707-46.703-62.707   c-25.473,0-40.632,17.142-47.301,33.724c-2.432,5.928-3.058,14.179-3.058,22.477V420.56h-92.219c0,0,1.242-251.285,0-277.32h92.21   v39.309c-0.187,0.294-0.43,0.611-0.606,0.896h0.606v-0.896c12.251-18.869,34.13-45.824,83.102-45.824   C384.633,136.724,430.117,176.361,430.117,261.543z M52.183,9.558C20.635,9.558,0,30.251,0,57.463   c0,26.619,20.038,47.94,50.959,47.94h0.616c32.159,0,52.159-21.317,52.159-47.94C103.128,30.251,83.734,9.558,52.183,9.558z   M5.477,420.56h92.184v-277.32H5.477V420.56z"></path></g></svg><span class="sr-only">LinkedIn</span></a><a class="i9-c-social-icon" href="/speeches/2013/lessons-from-lars#"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="16" width="16" viewBox="0 0 512 512" fill="#ffffff" aria-label="twitter"><path d="M389.2 48h70.6L305.6 224.2 487 464H345L233.7 318.6 106.5 464H35.8L200.7 275.5 26.8 48H172.4L272.9 180.9 389.2 48zM364.4 421.8h39.1L151.1 88h-42L364.4 421.8z"></path></svg><span class="sr-only">Twitter</span></a></div></div></div><div id="article-start" class="col-12 col-lg-8"><div class="i9-c-rich-text-area"><p>I got my economics Ph.D. from Chicago in the summer of 1987&mdash;over  a quarter of a century ago. At that moment in time, my infant research agenda  was largely a spillover of Lars&rsquo; own powerful agenda. Over the intervening time  period, though, our career tracks have led us in different directions. Somewhat  to my surprise, by the end of my academic career in 2009, I generally found  myself being labeled as an economic theorist by most other economists. (I say  &ldquo;most other economists&rdquo; because I generally found that theorists themselves wanted  to label me something else!) And in 2009, I left the academe to become a  monetary policymaker. That&rsquo;s a step that Lars has not made&mdash;at least not yet. </p>
<p> So, Lars and I have taken different paths in our lives. Nonetheless,  Lars has been a role model for me throughout my professional life&mdash;a person  whose example I&rsquo;ve striven to follow. Through our interactions over the past 25  years and more, he has taught me many important lessons. I&rsquo;ll use my brief time  to talk about two of those lessons: the power of mathematics and the value of  persistence. </p>
<p> I&rsquo;ll start with the power of mathematics. Early on in his  time as my adviser, Lars gave me a page and a half of comments on what was to  become the main chapter of my dissertation. Unfortunately, I&rsquo;m not much of a  pack rat, and so I have not kept a copy of those comments&mdash;now, they&rsquo;d probably  be worth millions on eBay.</p>
<p> But one of Lars&rsquo; comments has stuck in my mind. It was  typically pithy: Use L&infin;, not L<sup style="font-size: 12px;">2</sup>. I&rsquo;m sure that most of you are having the same  reaction that I did at the time: What? Abandon the wonderful inner product  structure of L<sup style="font-size: 12px;">2</sup>? That&rsquo;s sure going to make the proofs a lot harder. I&rsquo;m  sure that you can all empathize with the deep longing for inner products that I  felt back then. </p>
<p> I went away and thought about this statement&mdash;use L&infin; and not L<sup style="font-size: 12px;">2</sup>. After  some period of time&mdash;really, no more than a decade or so&mdash;I figured out why Lars had  said what he said. So, when Mikhail Golosov, Aleh Tsyvinski and I wrote a paper  together on optimal taxation in the early 2000s, we followed the advice that  Lars had given me as a student and used L&infin; as the basis of our  mathematics. By doing so, we were able to show that our results were  surprisingly fundamental, in the sense that they could be derived in a wide  class of seemingly disparate economic models. </p>
<p> I think that this little story is illustrative of what I see  as a basic message from Lars&rsquo; research: It takes harder mathematics&mdash;in my  story, L&infin; math&mdash;to get closer to economic fundamentals. A lot  of economic and econometric analysis relies on a host of auxiliary assumptions  that really have nothing to do with economics&mdash;for example, assumptions about serially  independent errors. We can cross our fingers&mdash;but we have no way of knowing  whether these auxiliary assumptions are ultimately responsible for our results.</p>
<p> To a remarkable extent, Lars&rsquo; econometric and economic analyses  dispense with these auxiliary assumptions. Doing the analysis without those  assumptions requires harder math. But here&rsquo;s the true irony. Because the harder  math allows us to get rid of the technical assumptions, the harder math gets us  much closer to understanding the core implications of economics itself. And I  believe that this is exactly why the generalized method of moments has become  so widely used in economics and other social sciences. </p>
<p> Let me move on to the second of the lessons that I learned  from Lars that I want to mention today: the value of persistence. The power of Lars&rsquo; remarkably  assumption-free approach to asset pricing is now obvious to us. But I was there  almost at the beginning&mdash;and, 25 years ago, I remember that the approach seemed  overly abstract and mathy to many. It took persistence on Lars&rsquo; part to  overcome those sentiments and get his ideas the widespread acceptance that they  now enjoy. </p>
<p> My own experience as a researcher was that persistence was  incredibly important. Whenever I came up with a new idea, I was told&mdash;by many  people&mdash;that the idea had to be wrong. Time would pass. And many of those same  folks would come and tell me that they had decided that the idea was not wrong.  Instead, they had a fresh criticism&mdash;they had decided that the idea was obvious  to the point of banality. For you young researchers out there, that&rsquo;s called &ldquo;winning  them over.&rdquo;</p>
<p> So, those are two of the many lessons that I learned from  Lars&mdash;the power of mathematics and the value of persistence. I&rsquo;ll close by  saying <em>why </em>I have been able to learn  those lessons from him. One reason is that, of course, Lars is remarkably  brilliant. But another is that Lars is remarkably generous with his time and  his thinking. I used to work at the University of Iowa back in the early &rsquo;90s. I  remember one of my colleagues there saying of Lars, &ldquo;Lars Hansen makes everyone  who ever talks to him a better economist.&rdquo; Enough said.</p>
<p>  Thanks, Lars.</p></div></div><div class="col-12 col-lg-2"></div></div></div><div class="container-fluid"><div class="row"><div class="container"><div class="i9-c-email-banner row no-gutters"><div class="i9-c-email-banner__content col-xs-12 col-lg-4"><h3 class="i9-c-email-banner__content-title">Sign up for news and events</h3><div class="i9-c-email-banner__content-description"><style>
